# Enforcing Security Invariants

Use this skill to review the security boundary actually changed. It does not
turn ordinary implementation into a full audit or require a separate session
for an authorized fix. Paths below refer to the active Registry Stack checkout.

## Start from the owning contract

| Surface | Current source of invariants |
| --- | --- |
| Evidence and wallet delivery | `products/evidence/contracts/security-invariant-matrix.yaml`, `security-test-traceability.yaml`, and the affected response or OID4VCI profile |
| Base Registry Engine | `products/breg/contracts/security-invariant-matrix.yaml` and `security-test-traceability.yaml` |
| Casework review and tasks | `products/casework/AGENTS.md`, `contracts/security-invariant-matrix.yaml`, and `contracts/security-test-traceability.yaml` under that product |
| Shared Review handoff | `crates/registry-review-protocol/README.md`, `crates/registry-review-client/README.md`, and the provider's owning contract |
| Coordinator admission, waits and recovery | `products/coordinator/AGENTS.md`, `products/coordinator/PRODUCT-CONTRACT.md`, and their named tests |
| Messaging provider effects and delivery receipts | `products/messaging/contracts/security-invariant-matrix.yaml`, `security-test-traceability.yaml` under that product, and `products/messaging/RUNTIME-CONFIG.md` |
| Scheduling capacity and task grants | `products/scheduling/contracts/security-invariant-matrix.yaml`, `security-test-traceability.yaml` under that product, and `products/scheduling/TASK_GRANTS.md` |
| Render inputs, deterministic output and isolation | `products/render/SECURITY-MATRIX.md`, `products/render/PAYLOAD.md`, and `products/render/ACCEPTANCE.md` |
| Discovery and selections | `products/discovery/contracts/security-invariant-matrix.yaml` and `security-test-traceability.yaml` |
| ThunderID issuer tooling | `crates/registry-thunderid-tooling/README.md` and the affected authentication, token, replay, or audit tests |
| Shared primitives | `products/platform/AGENTS.md`, changed crate README, and consuming product contracts |

Treat a planned guarantee differently from an enforced one. Read the owning
contract and named executable proof, not an old catalog of source line numbers.
Update any matrix row and its resolving test when the guarantee changes. Do not
weaken a named negative merely to accept the implementation.

## Review the changed path

Identify the actor and input, the asset or authority at risk, the enforcement
point, and a negative case that could falsify the guarantee. Distinguish
untrusted runtime data, reviewed hash-covered project artifacts, and privileged
operator configuration. A restrictive implementation technique is replaceable
when a simpler mechanism preserves the concrete invariant.

Weigh operator experience in every hardening decision. A check must not refuse
an ordinary deployment layout: a distribution log directory shared with a
system group, a container volume mount, or configuration management that
re-applies modes and ACLs. Prefer the narrowest check that blocks a threat
reachable by a runtime's actual adversary, and make every refusal name the
operator's fix. Fail closed wherever an audit record or persisted state could
otherwise be lost or misstated; that is not traded for convenience. Adopter
tooling (the `*ctl` binaries and issuer tooling) assumes a trusted single-user
development machine, so local filesystem races there are accepted risk;
runtimes are held to the full check.

Follow that input to its effect and release, including failure, cancellation,
retry, and concurrency paths that the change can reach. In particular:

- Authentication helpers and product principal/claim rules are distinct. Do
  not add fallback claims or union grants across profiles. Caller filters,
  selectors, headers, script output, and discovered metadata cannot manufacture
  authority.
- Review is optional where the source contract permits it. An ID-only policy
  selection delegates first-acceptance version choice to a trusted provider;
  a strict selection supplies both version and digest. Preserve the concrete
  accepted binding, original submission digest and result correlation across
  retries and activation changes. A provider result never replaces the source's
  independently authorized application and commit-time checks.
- Coordinator event admission and completion wake-ups have different effects.
  A trusted signed event may admit a run through an authored typed trigger;
  a completion wake-up only advances an eligible authoritative read. Preserve
  exact event identity and replay binding, and do not let admission, waits or
  recovery create downstream authority or renew an approval. The source owns
  event semantics and disclosure; the receiving product owns each effect.
- Task grants carry bounded human approval; the resource server still owns
  current authorization, expiry and commit checks under its documented status
  policy. Provider acceptance, confirmed delivery and an uncertain send are
  distinct outcomes; do not retry an uncertain external effect as fresh work.
- Governed packages and deployment bindings have different authority. Verify
  exact closure and activation before dependent effects, preserving the
  product's signature, identity, sequence, and recovery rules where applicable.
  A digest alone is not proof of an authorized signer.
- Authorization and durable attempt audit precede protected source effects;
  terminal audit gates exact bytes and successful mutations as the owning
  product specifies. Atomicity includes revisions, receipts, idempotency,
  events, and outbox state when those belong to the same transaction.
- Cached responses, history, pagination, saved selections, and replays retain
  their original context binding and obey current authorization or trust rules.
  Readiness and recovery must not silently bypass a failed migration, stale
  package, revoked key, or required review.
- Issuance, key-binding proof, request nonce, adapter token, and client assertion
  have different lifecycle rules. Equality or signature checks alone do not
  provide replay consumption. Preserve fail-closed bounds for live state.
- Protected existence, hidden inputs, and error timing can disclose data even
  when the response omits the field. Test the public-processing floor and
  indistinguishable outcomes required by the product.
- Secrets and subject/source values stay out of logs, public problems, metric
  labels, and generated public artifacts. Use synthetic canaries in failure
  paths to prove redaction rather than recording real sensitive data.

## Prove and report proportionately

Run the smallest permitted case and negative case that reach the changed
boundary. Use real PostgreSQL for database transaction/role/RLS guarantees;
use the real router for request ordering and response-contract guarantees.
Select affected broader checks through `running-dev-gates`. A changed developer
surface also needs a realistic authoring-to-use journey; security review alone
does not prove it is usable.

Record the invariant, enforcement point, executed proof, and any remaining
uncertainty. Before a PR, review the final diff against those specific claims.
Keep suspected vulnerabilities and private review evidence out of public
issues and PR prose; follow `SECURITY.md` for disclosure. Authorized private
investigation and fixes can continue within that boundary.

