that no facade binary links; only the companion executable and tests
depend on it. A connector calls the maintained product client only: never
hand-authored HTTP, never a product database, never a product runtime or
tooling crate. Each registered operation names, through a descriptor, its
input and output schemas, body kind (JSON or binary reference), product
binding, credential requirements, mutation and precondition behavior,
idempotency requirements, and recovery and completion rules. Only
implemented, enabled operations are advertised; an unsupported operation
fails during checking. The connector validates and constructs typed client
requests, supplies and persists command keys (client libraries never
invent them), and retains the client's prepared forms where they exist.

The operation contract, as a signature shape:

```text
describe() -> descriptor
prepare(input, binding, key) -> prepared   // exact, persistable, no bearer token
send(prepared) -> outcome                  // accepted | pending | completed | refused | uncertain
recover(prepared) -> outcome               // this operation's rule; never a new key
observe(correlation) -> native lifecycle state, where the family has one
```

Outcomes keep the product's native detail beneath the shared reporting
categories. Each family carries its own recovery rule from the client's
contract: BReg Start and Submit recover through `BRegPreparedAction` and
`BRegPreparedProposal`; the receipt-horizon refusals (`409
idempotency.key-reused`, `410 idempotency.expired`) surface as outcomes,
never as silent key changes; Scheduling's keyed capacity commands replay
by key while unkeyed release and busy reconciliation recover by snapshot
compare; Document Store follows its single-exchange-per-call rule.
Credentials and verified grant material stay host-owned and outside
mappings; a capacity command without a host-supplied task grant is
refused; destructive operations need a declared operation and an
appropriate grant, and source disappearance never selects one. Binary
content moves through an opaque host-owned content reference with
expected digest, length, and media type, never base64 through a mapping.

### 17.4 Custody, briefly

The ledger, sealing, erasure, and the delivery runner are the separate
brief's work. The contract here is the boundary: the engine's job entry
keeps no database of its own, the connector's prepared forms carry no
bearer token, and diagnostics stay value-free under `adapter.*` codes. A
later runner composes these pieces without widening any boundary this
section sets.

### 17.5 Dependency gate

`products/adapter/scripts/check_dependency_direction.py` names the
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

//! The generated JSON Schemas of the six Adapter formats, derived from the
//! source-neutral model in `registry-adapter-core` with `schemars` or from
//! the runtime's pure effective-contract projection, and
//! written to `products/adapter/schemas/` (`products/adapter/CONCEPT.md`
//! sections 6 and 12). The committed files are generated outputs: never
//! hand-edited, held to their sources by `adapterctl schemas --check`.

use clap::Args;
use registry_adapter_core::{
    ErrorClass, ADAPTER_FIXTURE_API_VERSION, ADAPTER_FIXTURE_KIND, ADAPTER_FIXTURE_SCHEMA_ID,
    ADAPTER_PACK_API_VERSION, ADAPTER_PACK_KIND, ADAPTER_PACK_SCHEMA_ID,
    ADAPTER_PROFILE_API_VERSION, ADAPTER_PROFILE_KIND, ADAPTER_PROFILE_SCHEMA_ID,
    ADAPTER_PROJECT_API_VERSION, ADAPTER_PROJECT_KIND, ADAPTER_PROJECT_SCHEMA_ID,
    ADAPTER_RUNTIME_API_VERSION, ADAPTER_RUNTIME_KIND, ADAPTER_RUNTIME_SCHEMA_ID,
};
use serde_json::Value;
use std::collections::{BTreeMap, BTreeSet};
use std::io;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

use crate::report;

/// The default directory the generated schemas are written to, relative to
/// the repository root the command runs from.
pub const DEFAULT_OUTPUT_DIR: &str = "products/adapter/schemas";

/// The static send URIs `SendUri::parse` accepts. Pinned to the parser by
/// the generator's own test.
const SEND_URI_PATTERN: &str = "^(direct|http|graphql):.+$";

#[derive(Debug, Args)]
pub struct SchemasArgs {
    /// Compare the generated bytes with the committed files and refuse on
    /// drift instead of writing anything.
    #[arg(long)]
    pub check: bool,

    /// Directory the generated schemas are written to or compared against.
    #[arg(long, value_name = "DIRECTORY", default_value_t = DEFAULT_OUTPUT_DIR.to_owned())]
    pub output_dir: String,
}

/// One generated schema: its file name, its derived document, and the
/// identity members the envelope adds.
struct Entry {
    file: &'static str,
    derived: Value,
    title: &'static str,
    schema_id: &'static str,
    api_version: &'static str,
    kind: &'static str,
}

fn entries() -> Result<Vec<Entry>, serde_json::Error> {
    use registry_adapter_core::{
        AdapterFixture, AdapterPack, AdapterProfile, AdapterProject, AdapterRuntimeConfig,
    };
    Ok(vec![
        Entry {
            file: "profile.v1alpha1.schema.json",
            derived: serde_json::to_value(schemars::schema_for!(AdapterProfile))?,
            title: "Registry Adapter facade profile",
            schema_id: ADAPTER_PROFILE_SCHEMA_ID,
            api_version: ADAPTER_PROFILE_API_VERSION,
            kind: ADAPTER_PROFILE_KIND,
        },
        Entry {
            file: "pack.v1alpha1.schema.json",
            derived: serde_json::to_value(schemars::schema_for!(AdapterPack))?,
            title: "Registry Adapter pack",
            schema_id: ADAPTER_PACK_SCHEMA_ID,
            api_version: ADAPTER_PACK_API_VERSION,
            kind: ADAPTER_PACK_KIND,
        },
        Entry {
            file: "project.v1alpha1.schema.json",
            derived: serde_json::to_value(schemars::schema_for!(AdapterProject))?,
            title: "Registry Adapter project",
            schema_id: ADAPTER_PROJECT_SCHEMA_ID,
            api_version: ADAPTER_PROJECT_API_VERSION,
            kind: ADAPTER_PROJECT_KIND,
        },
        Entry {
            file: "runtime.v1alpha1.schema.json",
            derived: serde_json::to_value(schemars::schema_for!(AdapterRuntimeConfig))?,
            title: "Registry Adapter runtime configuration",
            schema_id: ADAPTER_RUNTIME_SCHEMA_ID,
            api_version: ADAPTER_RUNTIME_API_VERSION,
            kind: ADAPTER_RUNTIME_KIND,
        },
        Entry {
            file: "fixture.v1alpha1.schema.json",
            derived: serde_json::to_value(schemars::schema_for!(AdapterFixture))?,
            title: "Registry Adapter fixture suite",
            schema_id: ADAPTER_FIXTURE_SCHEMA_ID,
            api_version: ADAPTER_FIXTURE_API_VERSION,
            kind: ADAPTER_FIXTURE_KIND,
        },
    ])
}

/// The six generated schema documents, keyed by file name. Deterministic:
/// the same workspace state renders the same bytes.
pub fn documents() -> Result<BTreeMap<&'static str, String>, serde_json::Error> {
    let shared: Value =
        serde_json::from_str(&registry_platform_config::schema::shared_blocks_document()?)?;
    let shared: BTreeSet<String> = shared
        .get("$defs")
        .and_then(Value::as_object)
        .map(|definitions| definitions.keys().cloned().collect())
        .unwrap_or_default();
    let mut documents = BTreeMap::new();
    for entry in entries()? {
        let mut derived = entry.derived;
        // The reader refuses `null` in every authored member (CFG-EMPTY-1),
        // so drop the `null` schemars adds to optional members; the platform
        // shared blocks keep the exact definitions the platform publishes.
        refuse_null_outside_shared_blocks(&mut derived, &shared);
        set_const(&mut derived, "apiVersion", entry.api_version);
        set_const(&mut derived, "kind", entry.kind);
        apply_manual_wire_forms(&mut derived, entry.file);
        let mut object = match derived {
            Value::Object(object) => object,
            _ => unreachable!("schemars derives a schema object for a struct"),
        };
        object.insert(
            "$schema".to_owned(),
            Value::String("https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema".to_owned()),
        );
        object.insert("$id".to_owned(), Value::String(entry.schema_id.to_owned()));
        object.insert("title".to_owned(), Value::String(entry.title.to_owned()));
        documents.insert(entry.file, render(&Value::Object(object))?);
    }
    documents.insert(
        "effective-contract.v1alpha1.schema.json",
        render(&registry_adapter::effective_contract::schema())?,
    );
    Ok(documents)
}

fn render(document: &Value) -> Result<String, serde_json::Error> {
    let mut rendered = serde_json::to_string_pretty(document)?;
    rendered.push('\n');
    Ok(rendered)
}

/// Drop the `null` branch schemars adds to each optional member, everywhere
/// except the shared-block definitions named in `shared`, which are embedded
/// exactly as the platform publishes them.
fn refuse_null_outside_shared_blocks(schema: &mut Value, shared: &BTreeSet<String>) {
    match schema {
        Value::Object(object) => {
            if let Some(definitions) = object.get_mut("$defs").and_then(Value::as_object_mut) {
                for (name, definition) in definitions.iter_mut() {
                    if !shared.contains(name) {
                        refuse_null(definition);
                    }
                }
            }
            for (key, member) in object.iter_mut() {
                if key != "$defs" {
                    refuse_null(member);
                }
            }
        }
        Value::Array(items) => items
            .iter_mut()
            .for_each(|item| refuse_null_outside_shared_blocks(item, shared)),
        _ => {}
    }
}

/// The reader refuses `null` in every member (CFG-EMPTY-1), so a schema must
/// not admit it either: remove the `null` branch from every `type` array and
/// `anyOf`, and a `default` of `null`.
fn refuse_null(schema: &mut Value) {
    match schema {
        Value::Object(object) => {
#!/bin/sh
# Prepare direct Cargo-built binary execution on macOS. AWS-LC FIPS is a
# dynamic library there, while Cargo only supplies its build directory to
# processes Cargo launches itself.

registry_cargo_build() {
  registry_stack_root=$1
  shift

  if [ "$(uname -s)" != Darwin ]; then
    cargo build "$@"
    return
  fi

  registry_cargo_messages=$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/registry-cargo-messages.XXXXXX") || return 1
  if cargo build "$@" --message-format=json-render-diagnostics >"$registry_cargo_messages"; then
    :
  else
    registry_cargo_status=$?
    python3 "$registry_stack_root/scripts/cargo_runtime_library_path.py" \
      --render-diagnostics "$registry_cargo_messages" >&2 || :
    rm -f -- "$registry_cargo_messages"
    return "$registry_cargo_status"
  fi
  registry_fips_library_path=$(
    python3 "$registry_stack_root/scripts/cargo_runtime_library_path.py" \
      "$registry_cargo_messages"
  ) || {
    registry_cargo_status=$?
    rm -f -- "$registry_cargo_messages"
    return "$registry_cargo_status"
  }
  rm -f -- "$registry_cargo_messages"

  case "$registry_fips_library_path" in
    *:*)
      echo "AWS-LC FIPS runtime directory cannot contain a colon: $registry_fips_library_path" >&2
      return 1
      ;;
  esac
  DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH="$registry_fips_library_path${DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH:+:$DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH}"
  export DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH
  REGISTRY_CARGO_RUNTIME_LIBRARY_PATH="$registry_fips_library_path"
  export REGISTRY_CARGO_RUNTIME_LIBRARY_PATH
}

registry_prepare_cargo_runtime() {
  if [ "$(uname -s)" != Darwin ]; then
    return 0
  fi
  registry_cargo_build "$@"
}

